Time complexity is a measure of the amount of time an algorithm takes to complete as a function of the input size (n). It helps evaluate the efficiency of an algorithm and predict its performance for large inputs.

Common Time Complexities:
O(1) - Constant Time: The execution time remains the same regardless of input size.

Example: Accessing an element in an array (arr[i]).

O(log n) - Logarithmic Time: The execution time increases logarithmically as input size grows.

Example: Binary Search.

O(n) - Linear Time: The execution time grows proportionally with input size.

Example: Traversing an array.

O(n log n) - Linearithmic Time: The execution time grows at a rate of n log n.

Example: Merge Sort, Quick Sort (average case).

O(n²) - Quadratic Time: The execution time grows proportionally to the square of the input size.

Example: Bubble Sort, Selection Sort.

O(2ⁿ) - Exponential Time: The execution time doubles with each additional input.

Example: Recursive Fibonacci calculation.

O(n!) - Factorial Time: The execution time grows factorially.

Example: Solving the Traveling Salesman Problem using brute fo

