Upper Bound & Lower Bound in Algorithm Analysis
When analyzing the time complexity of an algorithm, we define how its execution time grows as the input size n increases. This is done using upper bounds (Big-O) and lower bounds (Big-Omega, Ω).

1️⃣ Upper Bound (Big-O Notation)
Defines the worst-case time complexity.

It tells us the maximum time an algorithm will take for a given input size.

Ensures that the function never grows faster than a certain rate.

Example
Consider the function:
f(n) = 3n^2 + 5n + 2

The dominant term (the one that grows the fastest) is n^2.

So, Big-O (upper bound) is O(n^2).

📌 Mathematical Definition:
A function f(n) = O(g(n)) if there exist constants c > 0 and n0 such that:

