f(n) <= c \* g(n), for all n >= n0
Analogy
Think of an elevator with a weight limit. No matter what, the elevator won't carry more than the weight limit (this is the upper bound).

2️⃣ Lower Bound (Big-Omega Notation, Ω)
Defines the best-case time complexity.

It tells us the minimum time an algorithm will take for a given input size.

Ensures that the function at least grows at a certain rate.

Example
For the same function:
f(n) = 3n^2 + 5n + 2

The algorithm will always take at least Ω(n^2) operations.

📌 Mathematical Definition:
A function f(n) = Ω(g(n)) if there exist constants c > 0 and n0 such that:

r
Copy
Edit
f(n) >= c \* g(n), for all n >= n0
Analogy
Think of a speed limit on a road. No matter what, a car must at least move at the minimum speed (this is the lower bound).

3️⃣ Tight Bound (Big-Theta Notation, Θ)
When a function has both an upper and lower bound that match, we use Θ notation.

This gives a tight bound (exact order of growth).

📌 Mathematical Definition:
A function f(n) = Θ(g(n)) if there exist constants c1, c2 > 0 and n0 such that:

